def manuscript():
    B.manuscript();path=P/'TRA-20261014-V3.tex';t=path.read_text(encoding='utf-8');sr=R['strict'];fl=D['floors'];cap=[next(r for r in R['tradeoffs'] if r['case_id']==ci and r['lever']=='Tighter cap 1.10') for ci in CIS]
    abstract="Income polarization and cheaper road travel alter railway pricing, service and vulnerable travelers' participation. We formulate a railway design problem linking integer departures and fares to elastic participation and nested mode, product and seat choice. Three calibrated Chinese corridors reveal a conflict between commercial pricing authority and mobility. Central joint control raises profit while lowering railway-centered welfare as fares increase and service consolidates. Tightening the fare cap from 1.20 to 1.10 reduces commercial welfare losses by "+B.rng([100*r['welfare_loss_reduction'] for r in cap],0)+" percent, but does not eliminate them. A strict participation floor instead delivers positive welfare and attains "+B.rng([100*r['benchmark_fraction'] for r in sr[:2]],0)+" percent of the certified welfare benchmarks in the two single-OD corridors, retaining "+B.rng([100*r['profit_retained'] for r in sr[:2]],0)+" percent of commercial profit gains. Its performance is weaker in the multi-OD network. Conventional additions can support profitable incumbent adjustments while lowering traveler surplus; their welfare value depends on schedule convenience and newly served periods. Profit sacrifice per restored vulnerable trip is an operator budget ratio for a response benefiting travelers more broadly. Fare and participation regulation therefore provide complementary planning instruments."
